#!/usr/sbin/nft -f
#
# NFT-3b — Stage-3 OUTPUT-chain containment for ds-tlsproxy upstream egress (D76).
# artifact-version: 1
# governing: doc 12 §4.2 (the D76 kernel defense-in-depth layer) · doc 09 §5 (TLS-1)
#            · doc 14 §5 (mark-space layout, mask discipline, flush_session)
#            · doc 06 §3(c) (bugged-deputy containment) · D44 D69 D72 D73 D76
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#   The versioned, declarative Stage-3 OUTPUT-chain enforcement layer. It bounds
#   the proxy's *own* host-egress (the marked upstream leg) to the union of the
#   per-session admitted IPs ds-dnsgate already authorizes, and turns an
#   unmarked or unauthorized connect from the proxy UID into a fail-closed
#   drop + nflog event. It is a BUILD ARTIFACT installed by a systemd unit, never
#   hand-applied state — if you are typing `nft` into a host shell, you are doing
#   it wrong (doc 09 §3).
#
#   Like NFT-4 it lives in its OWN `inet` table (`ds_proxy_out`) so it layers on
#   top of the NFT-1 floor without re-authoring it: NFT-1's `output` chain stays
#   `policy accept` (host egress unconstrained at Stage 1, doc 09 §3), and this
#   Stage-3 containment rides here, reversible with a single
#   `nft delete table inet ds_proxy_out`. Removing it returns host egress to the
#   NFT-1 baseline ("connect anywhere") — the layer never makes things worse than
#   the no-layer baseline (doc 12 §4.2, the D76 honest-bug-containment framing).
#
# WHY THIS LAYER (doc 12 §4.2, D76 — non-gating Stage-3 hardening, off M1 gate)
#   It is honest bug containment + kernel-attributed audit, NOT compromise
#   containment: a full RCE in the proxy holds credential-swap material and is
#   catastrophic regardless, and a CAP_NET_RAW AF_PACKET egress bypasses the inet
#   OUTPUT chain entirely. What it buys: bounds the highest-likelihood bug surface
#   (the bespoke TLS-1 SNI-peek / re-admission, TLS-2 CONNECT, TLS-5 swap
#   upstream-selection code — the confused-deputy / SSRF shape) to ∪(admitted IPs
#   across sessions), which excludes RFC1918 / link-local / host ranges by DNS-4
#   construction; and the marked upstream leg gives LOG-2 kernel flow records for
#   the proxy's host-egress, closing LOG-4's uplink-reconciliation hole.
#
# SCOPE TO THE PROXY UID (doc 12 §4.2)
#   The chain is scoped to ds-tlsproxy's UID via `meta skuid`. Host-originated
#   egress from any OTHER uid (the host agent, ds-dnsgate's own resolution leg,
#   package mirrors, ops access) is not this layer's concern — it falls through
#   to NFT-1's accepting `output` chain untouched (this table only ever drops a
#   packet it explicitly owns; everything it does not match is `accept`-by-policy
#   here so non-proxy egress is never affected). The proxy user is install-time
#   data: the `$proxy_uid` define below is resolved when the artifact ships, the
#   same way the per-session tap index and allow-set names are runtime data, not
#   baked into the source. The CAP_NET_RAW-only / never-CAP_NET_ADMIN posture for
#   that UID is the host-baseline obligation (doc 12 §4.2, ../host-baseline/),
#   consumed here by reference: a compromised proxy must not be able to rewrite
#   the very ruleset that contains it (only ds-dnsgate / the host agent writes
#   nftables objects).
#
# MARKS (D76, doc 14 §5) — STRICT MASK DISCIPLINE
#   Every match of a DS mark goes through the explicit `& DS_MARK_MASK` clamp
#   (the Tailscale PR-5606 lesson, doc 14 §5). The ONLY mark literal that appears
#   in this file is the frozen `DS_MARK_MASK` mask constant itself, written as a
#   define sourced from `ds-contracts` (ds_contracts::mark::DS_MARK_MASK =
#   0xFF003FFF, doc 14 §5). No mark *value* is ever typed inline: the per-session
#   `0x2` ds-tlsproxy-upstream marks (ds_contracts::mark::Leg::TlsproxyUpstream)
#   are vmap KEYS added at runtime by ds-nft from the constants, and the `0x4`
#   infrastructure-egress mark (ds_contracts::mark::Leg::InfraEgress) is matched
#   via the symbol-fed `@infra_out_marks` set, never an inline literal. This file
#   touches NO bit in the permanently-unclaimed gap 23–14 (doc 14 §5) and writes
#   no mark register — it only MATCHES marks the upstream-socket SO_MARK path
#   (ds-tlsproxy, mark-before-connect) already set. The CI mark lint
#   (ds-contracts ds-nft-mark-lint, scripts/lint-nft-artifacts.sh) enforces all
#   of this.
#
# FAMILY (D75, doc 14 §11)
#   Authored `inet` so the IPv6 twins are dropped by the dormant-v6 default. The
#   per-session vmap dispatches to the v4 allow-set (`@allow4_<session>`) today;
#   the `@allow6_<session>` twin (doc 09 §3 NFT-3) stays dormant until v6 turns
#   on end to end (DNS-1/OQ10), at which point the per-session chain template
#   below grows its `ip6 daddr @allow6_<session>` line.
#
# SETS / MAPS / PER-SESSION CHAINS ARE RUNTIME STATE (NFT-6, doc 14 §5)
#   This artifact ships the EMPTY map/set skeletons and the documented per-session
#   chain SHAPE; the per-session `out_<session>` chains, the `@session_out` vmap
#   elements (keyed on the `0x2`-leg mark), and the `@infra_out_marks` element are
#   added / removed by `ds-nft` on the SAME atomic session create / destroy
#   transactions as NFT-6 (doc 12 §4.2: "vmap element add/remove rides the same
#   atomic session create/destroy transactions as NFT-6"). The allow-set CONTENT
#   (`@allow4_<session>`) is owned solely by ds-dnsgate (doc 09 §3 NFT-3) — this
#   table REUSES those sets by name (single source of truth, no data
#   duplication), it never writes an element into them.
#
# PARSE-CHECK (doc 06 (c) cadence)
#   `nft -c -f nft-3b-output.nft` is a full kernel-aware check; the `log`/`nflog`
#   and `vmap` expressions bind to the kernel even in check-only mode, so in a
#   container/sandbox without the modules loadable (or without a private netns)
#   `-c` may report a cache-init / "Could not process rule" ceiling — an
#   environment limit, not a grammar failure (the same ceiling
#   nft-4-resolver-closure.nft documents). Run on a host with the modules,
#   or `unshare -rn nft -c -f nft-3b-output.nft` for a private-netns grammar
#   check. Verify the unit with
#   `systemd-analyze verify ds-nft-output-bootstrap.service`.

# Install-time data: the proxy UID. Resolved when the artifact ships into the
# boundary image (the ds-tlsproxy service user), the same class of runtime data
# as the per-session tap index. The numeric default below is a strawman; the
# image build substitutes the real uid. This is NOT a mark literal — it is a
# uid selector for `meta skuid`.
define proxy_uid = 0xFFFFFFFE	# 4294967294 (nobody-class strawman; image build substitutes the ds-tlsproxy uid)

# The frozen DS mark mask (ds_contracts::mark::DS_MARK_MASK, doc 14 §5, D76).
# This is the ONLY mark literal allowed inline — it is the structural mask, not a
# mark value. Mark VALUES (the 0x2 upstream-leg and 0x4 infra-leg marks) are
# fed as vmap keys / set elements from ds-contracts at runtime, never typed here.
define DS_MARK_MASK = 0xFF003FFF

# Idempotent install: drop and recreate ONLY our own table. We never touch
# ds_boundary (NFT-1), ds_resolver_closure (NFT-4), or any other netns table.
table inet ds_proxy_out
delete table inet ds_proxy_out

table inet ds_proxy_out {

	# ---- Per-session OUTPUT dispatch vmap (NFT-6 runtime state) ----------------
	# Keyed on the masked DS mark; the value is the per-session `out_<session>`
	# verdict chain (a `jump`). ds-nft adds an element
	#   { <0x2-leg mark for session S> : jump out_<S> }
	# on session create and removes it on destroy, in the same atomic transaction
	# as the NFT-6 teardown (doc 12 §4.2). The mark KEYS come from ds-contracts
	# (ds_contracts::mark::compose(Leg::TlsproxyUpstream, host_session_index)) —
	# never authored inline here, so no mark value literal appears in this file.
	# Ships EMPTY: a freshly bootstrapped host has no sessions.
	map session_out {
		type mark : verdict
	}

	# ---- Infrastructure-egress marks (NFT-6 runtime state) ---------------------
	# The set of `0x4` infrastructure-egress marks (ds_contracts::mark::Leg::
	# InfraEgress) the proxy is permitted to use for its own infra traffic (e.g. a
	# flowlog ship / policy-subscriber leg that legitimately runs under the proxy
	# uid). Symbol-fed from ds-contracts so no `0x4` mark VALUE is ever typed
	# inline. Ships EMPTY; populated only if the deployment runs such a leg under
	# the proxy uid.
	set infra_out_marks {
		type mark
	}

	# ---- The OUTPUT enforcement chain -----------------------------------------
	# Scoped to the proxy UID. Default DROP: an unmarked or unauthorized connect
	# from the proxy is a fail-closed drop + nflog event (doc 12 §4.2: "an
	# unmarked connect from the proxy UID is a bug; once the OUTPUT chain lands it
	# is a fail-closed drop + nflog event").
	chain proxy_output {
		type filter hook output priority filter; policy accept;

		# Everything that is NOT the proxy uid leaves this layer alone — it falls
		# through to NFT-1's accepting output chain. We only ever scope rules to
		# the proxy uid below; this table imposes nothing on other host egress.
		meta skuid != $proxy_uid accept

		# (1) Return / already-established proxy flows ride conntrack and are
		#     never re-checked against the allow-set (the upstream leg of an
		#     admitted flow stays up even if the admission entry later expires —
		#     the doc 09 §3 / D68 expiry-gates-new-flows-only property). This is
		#     the OUTPUT-side twin of NFT-1's established/related accept.
		ct state established,related accept

		# (2) Per-session dispatch: clamp the mark with the frozen mask and look
		#     the `0x2` upstream-leg mark up in @session_out, jumping to the
		#     session's own `out_<session>` verdict chain. Mask discipline is
		#     explicit (`& $DS_MARK_MASK`) — the only mark literal in the file is
		#     the mask itself; the per-session mark KEYS are runtime vmap state
		#     from ds-contracts (doc 14 §5). A NEW proxy flow whose mark is not in
		#     the map (no live session, or an unmarked socket) falls through to
		#     the default drop below — fail-closed.
		meta mark & $DS_MARK_MASK vmap @session_out

		# (4) Infrastructure-egress accept: a `0x4` infra-leg mark the proxy is
		#     permitted to carry (flowlog ship / policy subscriber under the proxy
		#     uid, if any) is accepted. Matched against the symbol-fed
		#     @infra_out_marks set under the same explicit mask — no inline 0x4
		#     value (doc 14 §5). Empty by default, so this is a no-op until a
		#     deployment populates it.
		meta mark & $DS_MARK_MASK @infra_out_marks accept

		# (5) Default fail-closed: an unmarked / unauthorized connect from the
		#     proxy uid is a BUG (every upstream socket must carry a DS mark
		#     before connect — doc 12 §4.2, mark-before-connect, frozen). nflog it
		#     to group 2 (the LOG-2 upstream-leg stream ds-flowlog consumes) for
		#     kernel-attributed audit, then drop. This is the bugged-deputy
		#     containment backstop (doc 06 §3(c)).
		log prefix "ds-nft3b proxy-out-default-drop " group 2 counter drop
	}

	# ---- Per-session OUTPUT verdict chain TEMPLATE (NFT-6 runtime state) -------
	# DOCUMENTED SHAPE ONLY — not instantiated in this artifact. On session
	# create ds-nft adds a regular chain `out_<session>` with the body below and a
	# `@session_out` vmap element keying that session's `0x2` mark to `jump
	# out_<session>`; on destroy it removes both, atomically with the NFT-6
	# teardown (doc 12 §4.2). The chain REUSES ds-dnsgate's NFT-3 per-session
	# allow-set `@allow4_<session>` by name (single source of truth — this table
	# never writes an element into it):
	#
	#   chain out_<session> {
	#       ip daddr @allow4_<session> accept
	#       # (v6 twin, dormant until DNS-1/OQ10 turns IPv6 on end to end:)
	#       # ip6 daddr @allow6_<session> accept
	#       log prefix "ds-nft3b out-<session>-drop " group 2 counter drop
	#   }
	#
	# A marked upstream connect to an admitted IP traverses the allow-set and is
	# accepted; a connect to a denied public IP or an RFC1918 address (which the
	# allow-set never contains by DNS-4 construction) hits the per-session
	# `log ... drop`, nflog'd and attributed to the correct session (the doc 06
	# §3(c) bugged-deputy-containment assertion).
}
